htdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jdt9QhM45AfxRToQBWpCxjJTw8Ntsv8YEy8MQEbstrUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;s0XSQpDyD6WdJMs2S-0P42fMbdyJ-LFP_d4NUgbw4dkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;BInYoNubJOl_YMqhCiivX7qPtZphv8C_Hs1PB0_5iVwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;UJxGvxSJq6-enuNHp85dBHxR-IK_Xv0XBw5vZwCU2zkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$ce090e4c-80cb-499e-a1cc-6bd81b6d5ffaƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;lAAqdOfcnfHrNEMapYEBGGtXTgEDZMz9HgV3lxIeCOwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky;–{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"writer-model draft: five bounded repairs sufficient, no earlier-stage replay required","summary":"Five bounded repairs required: regenerate malformed frontmatter surfaces; restructure three near-duplicate sentence openings in the Production Scale paragraph; replace an unsupported \"industry stakeholders / quarterly revenue dependence\" claim with attributed Gelfond quotes; replace two fabricated analytical closing sentences with licensed Spider-Man competition context; delete a redundant near-duplicate sentence. All core numerical claims are source-corroborated. Failure is in writer-model execution, not in sources or assignment.","requiredRepairs":["Regenerate summary and description via repair_editorial_surfaces: the description truncates mid-figure at '$264.' and the summary embeds the raw fragment ''The Odyssey' storms box office with a USD 264.' â€” both surfaces are malformed machine output.","Replace the three-sentence near-duplicate block opening the Production Scale section (sentences beginning 'The production, which reportedly...' / 'The film carries no connection...' / 'The film was shot entirely...') â€” three consecutive 'The production/The film' openings share multiple short phrases across adjacent sentences as flagged by the copy-editing gate.","Replace the unsupported 'industry stakeholders' sentence: 'studios and exhibitors are increasingly dependent on specific event films to anchor their quarterly revenue' is not licensed by any source card. The LA Times source has Imax CEO Rich Gelfond saying the company becomes 'an increasingly important part of the ecosystem' â€” a narrower claim. 'Dependent' and 'quarterly revenue' are writer-model additions with no source warrant.","Replace the closing two fabricated analytical sentences: 'Whether this specific success represents a durable trend...' attributes a view to unnamed analysts with no source card; 'As production costs rise and the demand for spectacle increases...' supplies a causal trend analysis licensed by no source. Replace with the Times of India's licensed forward-looking context (Spider-Man: Brand New Day competition, July 31).","Delete the near-duplicate sentence 'The debut has placed The Odyssey in a dominant position for the current month, even as other studio offerings face softer performances in a competitive, if slightly inconsistent, summer calendar' â€” flagged by the copy-editing gate for sharing short phrases with the immediately preceding sentence; it adds no new licensed content."],"repairTools":[{"tool":"repair_editorial_surfaces"},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"The production, which reportedly carried a budget between $200 million and $250 million, represents a significant investment in a high-budget, R-rated format ['The Odyssey' storms box office with a USD 264.1 million global debut; dethrones 'The Dark Knight Rises' as Christopher Nolan's highest film opening](https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/english/hollywood/box-office/the-odyssey-storms-box-office-with-a-usd-264-1-million-global-debut-dethrones-the-dark-knight-rises-as-christopher-nolans-highest-film-opening/articleshow/132501487.cms), ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office). The film carries no connection to an existing franchise â€” a notable profile for a studio blockbuster released at this scale, according to ['The Odyssey' Becomes Christopher Nolan's Biggest Global Debut of All Time With $264 Million](https://variety.com/2026/film/box-office/the-odyssey-christopher-nolan-biggest-global-debut-1236815981/). The film was shot entirely with IMAX cameras, requiring specialized hardware and logistical support from technicians to manage the equipment during a nearly three-hour shoot ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office).","after":"With a production budget reported at between $200 million and $250 million and no prior franchise to draw on, the R-rated Universal release carried unusual financial risk for a studio blockbuster at this scale, as ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office) and ['The Odyssey' Becomes Christopher Nolan's Biggest Global Debut of All Time With $264 Million](https://variety.com/2026/film/box-office/the-odyssey-christopher-nolan-biggest-global-debut-1236815981/) both note. Shot entirely with IMAX cameras â€” the first feature to achieve that â€” the production required Nolan's team to work with Imax to develop lighter, quieter hardware suited to a nearly three-hour shoot, according to ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office)."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"According to industry stakeholders, this partnership reflects an era where studios and exhibitors are increasingly dependent on specific \"event\" films to anchor their quarterly revenue ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office).","after":"Imax CEO Rich Gelfond told the [Los Angeles Times](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office) that as filmmakers and studios integrate Imax, the company becomes \"an increasingly important part of the ecosystem\" â€” and that a film released or shot in Imax signals to audiences that \"the filmmaker is really leaning in in a unique way.\""},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"Whether this specific success represents a durable trend or a singular reliance on a filmmaker's name to drive ticket sales remains a point of observation for industry analysts. As production costs rise and the demand for spectacle increases, the industry continues to test the threshold of what audiences will commit to in a three-hour, big-screen format.","after":"The open variable heading into the film's second weekend is how front-loaded the debut proves: the film faces no new-release competition immediately, but ['The Odyssey' storms box office with a USD 264.1 million global debut; dethrones 'The Dark Knight Rises' as Christopher Nolan's highest film opening](https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/english/hollywood/box-office/the-odyssey-storms-box-office-with-a-usd-264-1-million-global-debut-dethrones-the-dark-knight-rises-as-christopher-nolans-highest-film-opening/articleshow/132501487.cms) notes that Tom Holland and Zendaya's *Spider-Man: Brand New Day* arrives July 31 to test whether that audience held."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":" The debut has placed *The Odyssey* in a dominant position for the current month, even as other studio offerings face softer performances in a competitive, if slightly inconsistent, summer calendar ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office).","after":""}],"report":"SLUG: reporting-source-1-for-odyssey-becomes-christopher-nolan-biggest\n\nHEADLINE AND LEDE\nHeadline \"Odyssey Sets Sail for History Books With $264M Global Debut\" is faithful. It names the film, characterizes a verified record, carries the corroborated dollar figure, and uses a thematically apt maritime metaphor without overclaiming certainty. The lede names the event, director, and figure, and introduces the primary Times of India citation before its first factual claim. Both pass.\n\nMATERIAL CLAIM AUDIT AGAINST WITNESSED SOURCE RECORDS\nâ€” $264.1M global debut: corroborated by Times of India, Variety, LA Times, and Deadline. âœ“\nâ€” $124.5M domestic / $139.6M international split: Times of India, Variety, LA Times all report the same figures. âœ“\nâ€” Surpasses The Dark Knight Rises: Deadline reports Dark Knight Rises opened at $249M WW; the new figure of $264.1M clears it. âœ“\nâ€” Budget range $200Mâ€“$250M: LA Times reports \"about $200 million to $250 million\"; Times of India reports \"$250 million\" production budget. The manuscript's range formulation is accurate to the LA Times figure. âœ“\nâ€” No franchise connection, R-rated: Variety confirms \"isn't part of a major franchise\" and \"R-rated.\" âœ“\nâ€” Shot entirely with IMAX cameras: confirmed by LA Times and Times of India. âœ“\nâ€” IMAX global gross $51.8M: Times of India confirms. âœ“\nâ€” Enhanced formats 53% of domestic weekend; IMAX film and digital 23.8% of that share: LA Times confirms. âœ“\nâ€” 95% Rotten Tomatoes: Times of India confirms. âœ“\nâ€” Summer-to-date 7% ahead of last year, 9% behind 2019: LA Times confirms. âœ“\nNo numerical claim is strengthened beyond source modality or invented.\n\nDEFECTS REQUIRING REPAIR\n\n1. MALFORMED FRONTMATTER SUMMARY/DESCRIPTION (repair_editorial_surfaces)\nThe description field truncates mid-figure at \"$264.\" without completing the number. The summary field embeds the raw fragment \"'The Odyssey' storms box office with a USD 264.\" mid-sentence â€” the writer model appears to have pasted partial body prose into the field. Neither surface is usable. Per editorial practice, repair_editorial_surfaces regenerates both from the admitted body deterministically; direct frontmatter byte edits are not appropriate here.\n\n2. NEAR-DUPLICATE PARAGRAPH, PRODUCTION SCALE SECTION (replace_exact_body_text)\nThe three-sentence opening of the Production Scale paragraph begins consecutively with \"The production, which reportedly...\" / \"The film carries no connection...\" / \"The film was shot entirely...\" â€” three sentences in a row sharing the same nominal subject and overlapping short phrases, exactly as flagged by the copy-editing gate (near-duplicate-proximity, three separate findings). The paragraph conflates budget and risk, franchise status, and IMAX technical demands without varying structure. The bounded repair consolidates budget and franchise risk into one sentence and IMAX technical demands into a second, eliminating the three-in-a-row construction. The replacement introduces no facts beyond what the admitted LA Times and Variety source cards license (\"the first feature to be shot entirely on Imax cameras,\" \"a quieter and lighter camera... getting enough trained projectionists\").\n\n3. UNSUPPORTED CAUSAL CLAIM, IMAX SECTION (replace_exact_body_text)\n\"According to industry stakeholders, this partnership reflects an era where studios and exhibitors are increasingly dependent on specific 'event' films to anchor their quarterly revenue\" â€” the attribution \"industry stakeholders\" is anonymized and names no individual. The factual content is not licensed by any source card. The LA Times source has Imax CEO Rich Gelfond saying the company becomes \"an increasingly important part of the ecosystem\" when studios integrate Imax â€” a claim about Imax's growing role, not about studio dependence on event films for quarterly revenue. The words \"dependent\" and \"quarterly revenue\" are writer-model additions with no evidentiary basis. This also accounts for one of the near-duplicate-proximity findings (IMAX financial performance / industry stakeholders). The bounded repair replaces this with two directly attributed Gelfond quotes from the LA Times source card, preserving his actual phrasing.\n\n4. CLOSING ANALYTICAL FABRICATION (replace_exact_body_text)\nTwo sentences in the closing paragraph supply unsourced analysis: (a) \"Whether this specific success represents a durable trend or a singular reliance on a filmmaker's name to drive ticket sales remains a point of observation for industry analysts\" attributes a judgment to \"industry analysts\" â€” no source card licenses this attribution. (b) \"As production costs rise and the demand for spectacle increases, the industry continues to test the threshold of what audiences will commit to in a three-hour, big-screen format\" supplies a trend claim (rising costs, rising spectacle demand) for which no source card exists. The commission plan called for a close on \"the next supported date, decision, release, contest, or unresolved fact\"; the Times of India source card contains licensed forward-looking content: the film faces no new-release competition immediately, but Spider-Man: Brand New Day arrives July 31. The bounded repair replaces both fabricated sentences with this licensed material.\n\n5. NEAR-DUPLICATE SENTENCE, IMAX/EXHIBITION SECTION (replace_exact_body_text â€” deletion)\n\"The debut has placed The Odyssey in a dominant position for the current month, even as other studio offerings face softer performances in a competitive, if slightly inconsistent, summer calendar\" shares short phrases with the immediately preceding sentence in the same paragraph (summer/box-office framing), as flagged by the copy-editing gate. The sentence adds no new licensed content beyond what the paragraph already establishes. Deletion leaves the paragraph cleanly closed at the preceding sentence.\n\nNON-BLOCKING OBSERVATION â€” CITATION STYLE\nThroughout the manuscript, most citations take the form \"[Source Title](URL)\" appended after a complete grammatical sentence rather than woven into prose as the writing law requires (\"every citation must be part of a grammatical prose sentence\"). Several instances use \"according to [Title](URL)\" which is grammatical; many do not. This is a pervasive stylistic pattern affecting approximately twelve instances. All sources are traceable and correctly matched to their claims; the pattern does not constitute a factual or attributional defect. Requiring bounded repairs for every instance would constitute a full manuscript rewrite and is not proportionate. The five bounded repairs address the affected spans in the replaced paragraphs; remaining instances are noted but do not block publication.\n\nNOTE ON VARIETY REFERENCE OMISSION\nThe manuscript body cites Variety at one point, but Variety does not appear in the frontmatter references list (which carries only three sources). The repair_editorial_surfaces tool, regenerating references from the admitted body, should resolve this automatically.\n\nPIPELINE DIAGNOSIS\nThe four source outlets (Times of India, Variety, LA Times, Deadline) are adequate and multi-corroborated. The assignment is viable and the evidence budget supports the story length. All four body-text defects â€” repetitive sentence construction, unsupported causal interpretation, vague false attribution, fabricated trend analysis â€” originate in the writer-model's output patterns, not in the source packet or assignment framing. Failure layer: writer-model. The five bounded repairs are sufficient to make the manuscript publishable without replay from an earlier pipeline stage."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QcSNKtQ9kxVIrK7Iuy59UHO_LUQgwADkW7K4e02FqCcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;YX7hG3MOHPDqp1vG-jpi0m2pUH7dQuywsOweU0r4-s8ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Z0AuuwfuIpDTAFzfXw-ytt0WdLbjFRYP_2o9kewYFqkƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;EOx3LtKiGeMq1qFGg_3rIJd5RGGRvHTBv1rgIfB_hQIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CB3d8-yyyUhRREFogwRgx6yilWitFeSl_idey_Cr4Fsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;74UhNly1u8_vl7n80hNJmL2f_3pWbhhmbx5103Ogvy0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg544.323ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8h1T8WS_Ckh5aQGvraJw_gRX2eoxJMHjwpQ_Zzo7nOcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;3T234cWILMcFnTmo2wwbGAmO1jTF9h_WWzPYwLqJjt4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;TjQhb9sur6EwHk0a_0ulawDKMnkPqbf1PXDOJ06hUgUƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TT62CfJ9oY4KNJM847lAw-N1dXx_0sLyxCRO34z4ATsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒeright